{"execution_score":7,"aesthetic_score":7,"feedback":"The topographic contour concept lands well — stacked differential-growth rings rendered as identical pins clearly channels Donovan's accumulation logic, and the warm-white-on-dark palette is restrained. The pin height encoding (taller at peak, shorter at base) is a smart readability choice, but 6K instanced cylinders at r=0.035 reads visually sparse for a Donovan piece that should feel like overwhelming material density; push MAX_PINS to 20K+ and shrink PIN_RADIUS to 0.015 so the surface feels packed rather than dotted. The slow orbital camera is fine but the breathing oscillation on contour heights is barely perceptible — either amplify it so the landscape visibly inhales or remove it to save the compute. Consider sampling pins along the filled interior of each contour ring (not just the perimeter) so the terrain reads as a solid topographic surface rather than concentric wire loops with pins on them."}
