{"execution_score":8,"aesthetic_score":7,"feedback":"The idea lands well — differential growth rings rasterised into a heightfield of identical pins is a faithful Tara Donovan translation, and the topographic contour layering is legible within 2 seconds. The bone-white-on-near-black palette is restrained and appropriate. Two things to push: the breathing animation is subtle enough to feel like a screensaver wobble rather than earned motion — consider a slower, more localised wave (e.g. radial ripple from center outward) so the topography itself appears to shift like a living survey map. Second, the camera starts far enough that the individual pin identity is lost at default zoom; tighten the FOV or pull the camera closer so viewers immediately read 'thousands of identical cylinders' rather than a smooth surface, since that material-unit legibility is the core of Donovan's practice."}
